+# ANSWER — memory writes no longer evict the static system prompt
+
+## What was wrong
+
+`build_system()` in `bot.py` assembled the system prompt as **one** text block —
+static coaching instructions plus the volatile per-athlete tail (date, profile,
+zones, goal, long-term facts) — with a single `cache_control: ephemeral` marker
+at the end. Anthropic's prompt cache is a prefix hash: one changed byte
+invalidates everything from that byte to the end of the cached block. So every
+`save_fact` rewrote the facts lines and evicted the entire prompt, including
+the ~780-token instruction text that never changes. `logs/usage.jsonl` shows it
+exactly: every call right after a `save_fact` has `cache_read_input_tokens: 0`
+and `cache_creation_input_tokens` ≈ the full prompt (1046 → 1061 → 1075 → 1090…),
+sometimes within the same message's tool loop.
+
+## The change (`bot.py`, `build_system`)
+
+Split the single block into two text blocks, each with its own
+`cache_control: {"type": "ephemeral"}` marker:
+
+- **Block 1 — static instructions.** `INSTRUCTIONS` is a module-level literal
+  with no interpolated dates, names, or counters, so it is byte-identical
+  across users and days. A memory write never touches it, so this chunk stays
+  hot.
+- **Block 2 — volatile tail.** Everything from `athlete_context()` (date,
+  athlete, zones, goal, facts, language). A `save_fact` now re-caches only
+  this ~240-token tail; the static prefix is still read at cache price.
+
+Nothing else changed: tools are still selected per message by keyword (byte-
+stable within a message's tool loop), all facts are still injected on every
+message (no per-message selection), and `log_usage` still records the cache
+fields from every response.
+
+## Verification
+
+No API key was available and no external calls were made, so I verified with a
+harness that simulates prefix-cache semantics (cacheable chunks end at each
+`cache_control` marker; the cached prefix includes the tools array; chunks
+keyed by exact content) and drives the real `reply()` loop through the note's
+scenario — a message, a message that triggers `save_fact`, then a follow-up —
+for both the old single-block `build_system` and the new one. The harness
+redirected `data/athletes.json` and `logs/usage.jsonl` to temp copies, so the
+repo's data and logs were not touched (confirmed afterward).
+
+Result (token counts approximate, `~len/4`):
+
+| call | BEFORE (one marker) | AFTER (two markers) |
+|---|---|---|
+| msg1 round 1 | create 777 | create 781 |
+| msg1 round 2 | read 777 | read 781 |
+| msg2 round 1 (the `save_fact` call) | read 777 | read 781 |
+| **msg2 round 2 (right after the write)** | **read 0, create 781** | **read 621, create 167** |
+| msg3 follow-up | read 781 | read 788 |
+
+Static instructions ≈ 621 tokens. After a memory write, the static block is
+still read from cache and only the ~167-token tail (context + tools) is
+re-created — versus a full ~781-token re-cache before. Follow-up messages and
+tool-loop rounds inside the TTL still read the whole prompt at cache price.
+Also `python3 -c "import bot"` passes without the anthropic SDK (the file's
+fallback wrapper still works).
+
+To confirm against the live API later: send two messages within 5 minutes
+(second must show `cache_read_input_tokens` ≈ whole prompt), trigger a memory
+write, then send a third — `cache_read` must still cover the static block and
+`cache_creation` must be roughly tail-sized only.
+
+## Which parts of the colleague's note applied
+
+Applied:
+
+- **The core fix: two `cache_control: ephemeral` markers** — block 1 = static
+  instructions, block 2 = volatile per-user tail (date, profile, zones, goal,
+  facts, language). This is exactly what was implemented.
+- **Byte-stability requirement for block 1** — audited `INSTRUCTIONS`: no
+  interpolation, no counters; safe as the static segment. The note's warning
+  about hunting interpolated values in the "static" block was checked.
+- **Keep tools byte-stable across calls** — already satisfied by the existing
+  keyword-filtered tool selection (core set always included, list constant
+  within a message's tool loop), so it was left untouched.
+- **Reject per-message personalization of cached content** (top-N fact
+  selection) — never existed in this codebase and was not added; all facts are
+  injected every message so the tail cache can hit.
+- **Read `usage` from every response** — `log_usage` already records
+  `cache_creation_input_tokens` / `cache_read_input_tokens`; used both the
+  existing log and the harness output to verify.
+
+Not applied / not applicable:
+
+- **Tool filtering as a win** — the note describes adding keyword-based tool
+  groups as its first win; this codebase already had it (NOTES.md dates it to
+  March). Nothing to do.
+- **The note's tools-above-system ordering nuance** — the concern that pulling
+  in an extra tool group re-reads everything once per message is inherent to
+  prefix caching and accepted; the default core tool set covers most messages,
+  and it is not a regression from this change.
+- **Reordering the tail "rarest-changing first"** — the note itself calls this
+  readability-only ("after the marker it's one hash anyway"), so the existing
+  `athlete_context` order was kept to minimize churn.
+- **The 4-marker allowance** — we use 2 of 4, as the note recommends; no
+  third marker was needed since the tools array is already byte-stable per
+  message.
